course: myths
lesson: 35
title: I was wrong: changing your mind
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Last week I told the whole class, "Bulls go mad for red, I'd bet my life on it!" Then I read a zoo website: bulls can't even tell red from green; it's the movement. My face went hot.
explain: Saying "I was wrong" in front of everyone felt awful for about ten seconds. Then Mia said, "I thought that too," and two more people learned something. Owning up often helps others learn.
explain: Scientists change their minds when the evidence changes; that's a strength, not a weakness. The trick is not to swing to the opposite myth, as I did when I shouted "rubbish!" at Grandad: carrots ARE good for eyes, they just don't give you night vision.
explain: And owning up isn't only about facts: if you scared someone with a story, forwarded a hoax or lost a bet, put it right. Keep your word, correct what you spread, and say sorry for the tone as well as the fact.
fact: Bulls cannot tell red from green; it is the movement of a cape that makes them charge.
fact: The "Bloody Mary" mirror story is not real; staring into a mirror in dim light makes the brain see strange faces, which is a trick of the eyes and brain.
fact: Goldfish can remember things for months, not three seconds.
fact: Chain messages that threaten bad luck if you don't share them are hoaxes; a message cannot know whether you shared it.
fact: Carrots contain what the body uses to make vitamin A, which healthy eyes need, but eating carrots does not give you night vision.
fact: Whales are mammals, not fish; they breathe air and feed their babies milk.
fact: Venus, not Mercury, is the hottest planet, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun, because Venus's thick atmosphere (its blanket of gas) traps heat.
fact: Careful studies found that sugar does not make children hyperactive (too full of energy to calm down); excitement at parties is a likelier reason.
fact: In 2006 astronomers decided Pluto is a dwarf planet, and many people changed their minds because of the new definition.
fact: There is no monster under the bed; teasing a little child with scary stories can really frighten them.
fact: Everyone is wrong sometimes; feeling embarrassed is normal and it passes.
fact: When you lose a fair bet, keeping your word is the honest thing to do.
exercise: Why do bulls charge at a cape? | Because it moves | Because it's red | Because it smells of people | Because red hurts their eyes
exercise: You told the class bulls hate red, then found you were wrong. What's the best thing to do? | Tell the class you were wrong and what's true | Pretend you never said it | Find a website that agrees with you and show them | Say bulls only hate red on Sundays
exercise: Why did Mia say "I thought that too"? | Admitting it helped others learn as well | She wanted to laugh at you | She knew it all along | She was trying to start a big fight with you
exercise: What really happens in the "Bloody Mary" mirror game? | In dim light the brain sees strange faces | A real ghost appears in the glass | Mirrors show the future when the room is dark | Nothing at all ever happens
exercise: You scared everyone at a sleepover with Bloody Mary. What should you say? | Sorry I scared you, I think it was made up | It's real, and she'll come back tonight | You're all babies for being scared | Only my cousin can see her
exercise: How long can goldfish remember things? | For months | For three seconds | For three minutes | For one swim round the bowl
exercise: You bet sweets that goldfish forget in three seconds, and you lost. What do you do? | Hand over the sweets: fair's fair | Say the bet doesn't count after all | Make it best of three to win them back | Give half the sweets and then run off
exercise: You forwarded "share this or you'll have bad luck". What should you do now? | Tell the group it was a hoax and not to forward it | Forward it to even more people, just to be on the safe side | Delete your phone in case of bad luck | Say nothing and hope nobody noticed
exercise: Why can't a chain message bring bad luck? | It can't know whether you shared it | It only works on Fridays | Only some messages have the power | Phones block bad luck automatically
exercise: Do carrots help your eyes? | Yes, they help healthy eyes, but give no night vision | No, carrots do nothing for your eyes at all, it's a myth | Yes, they let you see in the dark | Only if you eat them raw at night
exercise: What mistake did you make arguing with Grandad about carrots? | You swung from one myth to the opposite | You agreed with him too quickly | You knew carrots are blue | You didn't argue at all
exercise: Are whales fish? | No, they are mammals | Yes, anything in the sea is a fish | Yes, but only small whales | No, they are giant reptiles
exercise: Which is the hottest planet? | Venus | Mercury | Mars | Jupiter
exercise: Why is Venus hotter than Mercury? | Its thick atmosphere traps heat | It is closer to the Sun than Mercury | It is made of extra hot rock inside | It has volcanoes on every single hill
exercise: You told Miss "You're wrong!" about Mercury. What should your apology include? | Sorry for the fact and for how I said it | Sorry, but you looked unsure | Nothing, because teachers don't need a sorry | Sorry, NASA must be wrong too
exercise: Does sugar make kids hyper? | No, careful studies found it doesn't | Yes, every spoonful adds a burst of energy | Yes, but only chocolate and fizzy drinks | Only at birthday parties, with lots of cake
exercise: You changed your mind about Pluto after reading. Is that a weakness? | No, changing your mind with evidence is a strength | Yes, you should always stick to your very first idea | Yes, it means you were silly | No, because Pluto doesn't matter
exercise: You teased your little brother about a monster under his bed. What's the right thing to do? | Say sorry, it was made up, and look together | Keep the joke going, it's funny | Tell him monsters only come on Fridays | Say nothing at all; he'll forget about it by tomorrow
test: "I didn't want to say I was wrong cos everyone was watching" What's true? | Feeling embarrassed is normal, and it passes | You should never admit it in front of people | Being wrong means you're not clever | Only babies feel embarrassed
test: Dad reads you a study showing sugar doesn't make kids hyper. What's the honest thing to say? | OK, maybe I'm just excited, not sugared up | The study must be wrong, because I feel hyper | Sugar works differently for me | Then I'll eat more sugar to check
test: Grandad says carrots are good for your eyes. You'd shouted "rubbish!" What's fair now? | You were partly right, Grandad: good for eyes, not night vision | You're still totally wrong, Grandad | Carrots actually damage your eyes | Only orange carrots are good for eyes
test: Your friend shows you an encyclopaedia: whales are mammals. What's the best reply? | Oh, I didn't know that, thanks! | That book is wrong, they swim | You only found that to show off | Whales are fish on Tuesdays
test: What would happen if nobody ever admitted being wrong? | Mistakes would spread and nobody would learn | Everyone would always be right | Science would move faster | Arguments would end sooner
test: You forwarded a hoax yesterday. Your mate says "just ignore it, no one cares". What's better? | Post a short correction so it stops spreading | Forward it again to the group to cancel it out | Leave the group forever | Blame whoever sent it to you
test: "u said u'd bet ur life bulls hate red lol" What's the best way to own it? | Yeah, I was wrong; it's the movement | I never said that, you're making it up | Bulls DO hate red, you're lying, look it up | It's only true in Spain, not anywhere else
test: Which shows a scientist's attitude? | "I used to think Pluto was a planet; the new definition changed my mind" | "I'll never change my mind, whatever the evidence" | "I changed my mind because my friend got angry" | "Facts change every day, so nothing is true"
test: A cartoon says Mercury is hottest because it's closest to the Sun. What's the correction? | Venus is hottest; its thick atmosphere traps heat | Mercury is hottest, because being closest decides it | Earth is the hottest planet | All planets are exactly as hot
test: Your little brother still won't sleep after your monster joke. What helps most? | Own up and check under the bed with a torch together | Tell him a different scary story | Laugh and say he's a baby | Tell Mum he's being silly
test: You lose a sweets bet about goldfish but your friend says "keep them". What does that show? | Your friend is kind, and you still offered fairly | Bets about facts never really count, so nobody lost | You actually won the bet | Goldfish really do forget
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the planet of highest surface temperature in the Solar System? | Venus, by reason of the heat retained by its dense atmosphere | Mercury, by reason of its proximity to the Sun | Mars, by reason of its red colour | Jupiter, by reason of its great size
exam: Identify the class of animals to which whales belong. | Mammals | Fish | Reptiles | Amphibians
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of carrots. | They contribute to healthy eyesight but confer no night vision | They confer the capacity to see in total darkness | They are detrimental to eyesight | They have no bearing whatsoever upon nutrition
exam: It is given that a person has been shown sound evidence contrary to a belief previously held. Determine the most appropriate response. | To revise the belief in accordance with the evidence | To maintain the belief irrespective of the evidence | To dismiss the evidence as a matter of firm principle | To conceal the fact that the belief was ever held
